<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alalakh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tell Atchana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alalakh is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> city mound at Tell Atchana near Antakya in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amuq-valley">Amuq plain</a>. It was the capital of the kingdom of Mukish, under Yamhad in the 18th century BCE and later under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Leonard Woolley</a>'s excavations from 1936 found palaces, temples and archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets, as well as the statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrimi">Idrimi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alalakh.jpg" alt="Alalakh" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fkitselis · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Archaeological_site_of_Alalakh_(Tell_Atchana).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Alalakh, now Tell Atchana, lies in the Amik Valley about 20 kilometres northeast of Antakya in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>'s Hatay Province, roughly 2 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>–Turkey border. The mound covers about 22 hectares and stands on the flood plain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orontes-river">Orontes River</a>, where it enters the Amuq Plain, about 700 metres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tell-tayinat">Tell Tayinat</a>. Settlements in the valley date back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> period, as early as 6000 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Alalakh was founded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amorites">Amorites</a> in the late third millennium BCE, and its first palace was built around 2000 BCE. In the 18th century BCE it belonged to the kingdom of Yamhad, whose centre was modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. Around 1750 BCE Abba-El I of Aleppo gave the city to his brother Yarim-Lim, and Alalakh became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of Mukiš. It was destroyed in the second half of the 17th century BCE, possibly by the Hittite king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a>.</p><p>After a gap of less than a century, written records resume. The statue inscription of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrimi">Idrimi</a> says that in the 15th century BCE he fled to Emar, lived seven years among the Hapiru, and took Alalakh by a sea attack. Scholars have long debated its historicity. In the mid-14th century BCE the Hittite Suppiluliuma I defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tushratta">Tushratta</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a> and took northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, including Alalakh. According to Eric Cline the city was largely abandoned by 1300 BCE. The site was reoccupied in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, but the port of Al Mina took its place.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-woolley">Sir Leonard Woolley</a> excavated Tell Atchana, assisted by the epigrapher Sidney Smith, and found palaces, temples, private houses and fortification walls in 17 levels. About 500 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets came from Level VII and Level IV. The Akkadian texts are mostly juridical tablets, recording the ruling family's control of land and income, and administrative records of goods passing in and out of the palace. The inscribed statue of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idrimi">Idrimi</a> is now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>.</p><p>From 2003 a team directed by K. Aslihan Yener, first from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a>, has excavated the site. It found more than 70 wall-painting fragments in the Level VII palace. According to Manfred Hutter, the goddess Kubaba was first worshipped in the Amik Valley, especially at Alalakh, and her cult then spread to Carchemish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alalakh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Barattarna is one of the earliest securely attested kings; around 1500 BCE he extended Mitanni authority over neighbouring states, including Aleppo and <b>Alalakh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mitanni-empire">Mitanni Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Further light comes from the inscriptions of Idrimi, the 15th-century BCE king of <b>Alalakh</b>, and the tablets of Emar of the 14th and 13th centuries BCE.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>Minoan-style wall paintings have also been found at Tell el-Dab’a in the Nile Delta, at Tel Kabri in present-day Israel and at <b>Alalakh</b> in Turkey, perhaps the work of Cretan painters employed by foreign rulers.</blockquote><a href="/culture/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Alalakh</b>, at Tell Atchana near Antakya, was a town of the fertile Amuq plain by the early 2nd millennium BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mukish">Kingdom of Mukish</a></li></ul></section>
